    relationshipLenses: [
      { context: 'dating', title: 'Dating', description: 'The Overloaded One approaches dating like a project. They plan the dates, manage the logistics, and carry the conversation. Their partner may feel swept up, but also invisible. The Overloaded One struggles to be present—they\'re too busy managing. They don\'t know how to be pursued or cared for. They may be resentful when the other person doesn\'t "do their share," but they haven\'t left room for anyone else to contribute.' },
      { context: 'partnership', title: 'Long-Term Partnership', description: 'Over time, they take on more and more of the partnership. They manage the household, finances, planning, and emotional labor. Their partner may become passive or withdraw, sensing there\'s no space for them to contribute. The Overloaded One resents this, even though they\'ve trained their partner to step back. Intimacy suffers because they cannot relax enough to be genuinely close.' },
      { context: 'friendships', title: 'Friendships', description: 'They are the friend who plans everything: the catch-ups, the trips, the logistics. They check in on others but rarely let others check in on them. Their friends may see them as the reliable one, not realizing the cost. The Overloaded One might cancel plans because they\'re overcommitted. They feel secretly disappointed that no one plans for them, but they\'ve never stopped planning to let someone else try.' },
      { context: 'parenting', title: 'Parenting', description: 'They over-parent. They plan every activity, schedule every hour, and manage every outcome. Their children may not develop their own agency because the Overloaded One is always there to anticipate and solve. They are exhausted, but they cannot stop. The children may feel loved but also smothered. The Overloaded One believes they are doing it all for their kids, but they\'re also driven by an inability to tolerate the chaos of letting kids be kids.' },
      { context: 'work', title: 'Work', description: 'They are the one who is always available, always working, always solving. They are the "go-to" person. They are praised and relied upon—but rarely promoted, because they are too valuable in their current role. They can\'t delegate because they believe no one can do it right. They burn out, but they keep pushing, because stopping would mean admitting the system is broken.' },
      { context: 'conflict', title: 'Conflict', description: 'They avoid conflict—not because they\'re afraid of confrontation, but because conflict is inefficient. It takes time. It creates problems they don\'t have time to solve. When conflict does arise, they try to manage it: fix it, solve it, or avoid it entirely. They may shut down emotionally and lean into a "get it done" mode, which frustrates the other person and leaves issues unresolved.' },
      { context: 'receiving', title: 'Receiving Care', description: 'Receiving is uncomfortable. It disrupts the pattern. They feel guilty when someone offers help. They say no, or they say yes but micromanage it. They feel that if someone else does it, it won\'t be done right. Receiving care means losing control, and control is their only source of safety. They may perform a kind of gratitude—but underneath, they\'re waiting for the other person to leave so they can reassert control.' },
    ],
