    wisdomPerspectives: [
      { id: 'buddhism', label: 'Buddhism', question: 'What attachment creates suffering here?', content: 'The Unheld One is attached to self-reliance as a shield against disappointment. They cling to the belief that they cannot need anyone. But this attachment is a prison. The fear of being let down controls them more than any external event. Buddhism points out that the belief in a self that must be "independent" is a delusion—all beings are interdependent. Suffering comes from resisting this truth.', reflection: 'What would it mean to allow yourself to be held, even for a moment? What is the fear beneath the resistance?' },
      { id: 'stoicism', label: 'Stoicism', question: 'What is truly within your control?', content: 'Stoicism teaches that we cannot control others\' actions, but we can control our own responses. The Unheld One has decided that they cannot control others, so they will control everything themselves. But this is a misunderstanding. The Stoic practice is to distinguish between what is up to us—our choices, our actions—and what is not. We can ask for help; we cannot control the response.', reflection: 'What would it mean to ask for what you need and release the outcome? What is the cost of refusing to take that risk?' },
      { id: 'somatic', label: 'Somatic Perspective', question: 'What is the body preparing for when it refuses to receive?', content: 'The body may be preparing for disappointment. It has learned that reaching out results in pain—not always, but often enough. The body holds a posture of readiness: a bracing against the possibility of being let down. The chest tightens. The shoulders pull forward. The breath stays shallow. This is a protective posture, but it is also a prison. The body cannot receive love in a posture of bracing.', reflection: 'What would it feel like to soften your chest? What would it be like to let your guard down, even for a moment?' },
      { id: 'chakra', label: 'Chakra and Yogic Tradition', question: 'Where might receiving feel difficult from the perspective of chakra traditions?', content: 'Within chakra traditions, the heart center is symbolically associated with giving and receiving, the throat with asking, and the root with safety and grounding. As a reflective framework, the Unheld One may find receiving more difficult than giving, or asking for support harder than offering it. These images are not claims about energy states—they are metaphors that can help someone ask where their capacity to accept support may be asking for attention.', reflection: 'What would it feel like to open your heart to receive? What would it be like to trust that you can be held without losing yourself?' },
      { id: 'symbolic', label: 'Symbolic Perspective', question: 'If this pattern were a story, what role would you be playing?', content: 'The Unheld One is the figure at the edge of the fire—watching the warmth, but staying cold. They are the one who stands in the doorway, one foot out, never fully coming in. They are the lost child who grew up, but still waits for someone to come and find them. The role is lonely, but it is also a story about waiting—waiting for proof that it is safe to belong.', reflection: 'What would it take for you to walk all the way into the room? What would you risk losing—and what might you gain?' },
      { id: 'mythology', label: 'Mythology', question: 'Which myth captures the isolation of the one who cannot be held?', content: 'The Unheld One is the figure of Penelope, weaving and unweaving by night, waiting for a return that may never come—but Penelope had hope. The Unheld One has given up hope. They are the one who pushes away the very thing they most desire. The myth repeats until someone decides to stop pushing and see what happens.', reflection: 'What would happen if you stopped pushing? What would it be like to let yourself be held, even just to see what it feels like?' },
    ],
    recoveryRoadmap: [
      { stage: 1, title: 'Notice the Isolation', goal: 'Recognize that you are alone by choice, not by circumstance.', description: 'The Unheld One may believe they are alone because they have been abandoned. The first stage is seeing that they are also choosing to remain alone—even when others are present. They must notice the moment they reject an offer of help, the moment they pull back from connection. This is not shame—it is awareness.', practice: 'The Withdrawal Notice: when you catch yourself rejecting help or pulling away, pause and simply notice. Don\'t judge it. Just see it.' },
      { stage: 2, title: 'Identify the First Caregiver Wound', goal: 'Connect the pattern to its origin without self-blame.', description: 'The Unheld One must trace their isolation back to early experiences. Who was the first person they learned not to rely on? This may be a parent, a caregiver, a sibling. This is not about blaming—it is about understanding that the pattern was a survival strategy. They did not choose to be this way. They were shaped.', practice: 'The Origin Letter: write to the person you learned not to rely on. Tell them what you needed then and why you stopped asking. This letter is not for sending—it is for your own understanding.' },
      { stage: 3, title: 'Practice Small Reaching', goal: 'Allow yourself to be held in small, low-risk ways.', description: 'Start with small reaches—a text to a friend, a comment in a meeting, a moment of leaning on someone literally. Learn that reaching is not always met with rejection. It is vulnerable, and it is terrifying, but it is also the only way out of isolation.', practice: 'The Small Reach: once a day, ask for something small—a cup of coffee, a brief conversation, help with a minor task. Practice being on the receiving end.' },
      { stage: 4, title: 'Tolerate the Fear of Disappointment', goal: 'Learn that disappointment is not catastrophic.', description: 'The Unheld One has a catastrophic view of disappointment. They have learned that being let down is unbearable. This stage is about building tolerance: asking someone to be there and accepting that they may not be. The world does not end. The fear is real, but the fear is not the reality.', practice: 'The Risk Taking: ask someone for something meaningful—a favor, a conversation, a presence. Notice the anxiety, and stay with it. If they say yes, you learn trust. If they say no, you learn you can survive it.' },
      { stage: 5, title: 'Choose Interdependence', goal: 'Move from survival isolation to chosen connection.', description: 'The final stage is not about becoming dependent—it is about choosing interdependence. You can be self-sufficient and still rely on others. You can hold yourself and be held. This is the paradox: you become most capable when you stop pretending you can do it alone.', practice: 'The Interdependence Practice: each week, name one person you are willing to depend on. Tell them you need them. Let them show up.' },
    ],
    originalQuotes: [
      'You did not learn to be alone because you were weak—you learned it because you had no choice.',
      'The person who says "I don\'t need anyone" has often needed someone more than anyone.',
      'You are not protecting yourself by refusing to need—you are protecting yourself from the fear of needing.',
      'The cost of not asking is carrying everything alone.',
      'Loneliness is not the absence of others. It is the absence of being held.',
      'You were not meant to carry everything by yourself.',
      'Reaching is not a weakness. It is the bravest thing you can do when you\'ve been let down.',
      'The door is not locked. You just forgot you had the key.',
    ],
