    wisdomPerspectives: [
      { id: 'buddhism', label: 'Buddhism', question: 'What attachment creates suffering here?', content: 'The Controller is attached to outcome—the belief that they can and must engineer the future. This attachment creates chronic anxiety because the future is inherently unknowable. Buddhism points to the futility of this grasping: the more you try to control, the more you suffer. The attachment is to a self that must be in charge, but the self is not fixed—it cannot hold everything.', reflection: 'What would happen if you released the need to know the outcome? What if you trusted that things could work out without your constant management?' },
      { id: 'stoicism', label: 'Stoicism', question: 'What is within your control, and what is not?', content: 'The Controller has confused their domain: they are trying to control things they cannot—others\' actions, outcomes, the future. Stoicism teaches a simple distinction: only your own judgment, choices, and intentions are within your control. You can plan, but you cannot ensure the plan works. You can influence, but you cannot force outcomes. Releasing the illusion of control is the beginning of peace.', reflection: 'What are you trying to control that is not yours to control? What if you limited your energy to your own actions?' },
      { id: 'somatic', label: 'Somatic Perspective', question: 'What is the body preparing for when it holds everything so tightly?', content: 'The body may be preparing for chaos. It has learned that the world is unpredictable and dangerous. The shoulders brace. The jaw clenches. The breath becomes shallow. This is not a choice—it is a somatic adaptation to a past where chaos was a threat. The body is living in a past that is no longer present. It does not know it is safe to relax.', reflection: 'Where do you feel control in your body? What would happen if you consciously released that grip for a moment?' },
      { id: 'chakra', label: 'Chakra and Yogic Tradition', question: 'Where is power and control out of balance?', content: 'Within chakra traditions, the solar plexus center is symbolically associated with personal will and direction, the heart with connection, and the root with grounded safety. As a reflective framework, the Controller may experience their energy as concentrated in will and management, while the sense of open connection or trust in support feels less available. These images are not claims about energy states—they are metaphors that can help someone ask where their attention is distributed and what may be asking for more balance.', reflection: 'What would it feel like to lead from the heart instead of the solar plexus? What would you trust if you didn\'t have to control it?' },
      { id: 'symbolic', label: 'Symbolic Perspective', question: 'If this pattern were a story, what role would you be playing?', content: 'The Controller is the General who refuses to surrender, even when the battle has ended. They are the one who stands guard at the gate, convinced that if they sleep, the enemy will enter. They are the captain who will not relinquish the wheel, even in calm seas. The role is exhausting, but they have forgotten that they can step down.', reflection: 'What would happen if you stopped guarding? What would you see if you allowed yourself to rest in the safety that is already present?' },
      { id: 'mythology', label: 'Mythology', question: 'Which myth captures the cost of constant control?', content: 'The myth of Sisyphus captures this pattern—the endless, futile effort of controlling what cannot be controlled. But there is also the story of Prometheus, who tried to outwit the gods and was bound to a rock. The Controller is bound to their own system. They have become a prisoner of their own vigilance.', reflection: 'What would it mean to be unchained? What would you do with the freedom of not having to manage everything?' },
    ],
    recoveryRoadmap: [
      { stage: 1, title: 'Notice the Urgency', goal: 'Recognize the somatic signal of control—the spike of anxiety that says "you must act now."', description: 'The Controller is driven by a sense of urgency that feels like necessity. The first stage is noticing this signal as a signal, not a truth. It\'s the body\'s anxiety, not the world\'s emergency. You can pause and observe it without acting on it.', practice: 'The Urgency Pause: when you feel the urge to correct or take over, pause for five breaths. Notice the sensation of urgency. You don\'t have to act on it.' },
      { stage: 2, title: 'Practice Delegation Without Monitoring', goal: 'Let someone else do something without checking, correcting, or taking back.', description: 'The Controller\'s growth involves letting others do things "wrong"—or simply differently. This is not about efficiency. It is about building trust in others and in the process. Let someone else plan the meal, drive the route, or lead the meeting. Let them do it their way.', practice: 'The Delegation Experiment: delegate one task per week. Do not check on it. Do not redo it. Let it be.' },
      { stage: 3, title: 'Tolerate the Uncertainty', goal: 'Build capacity to hold discomfort without immediately controlling it.', description: 'Uncertainty is the Controller\'s greatest fear. They must learn to sit with it—to tolerate the discomfort of not knowing, not fixing, not planning. This is a skill that builds over time. They can start small: an unplanned hour, a flexible plan, a moment of not knowing.', practice: 'The Uncertainty Practice: each day, intentionally create one small uncertainty in your day. Leave one decision unmade. Notice the anxiety and stay with it.' },
      { stage: 4, title: 'Redefine Power', goal: 'Shift from controlling outcomes to influencing with presence and trust.', description: 'Power is not control—it is influence. The Controller must learn that their power lies in trust, presence, and collaboration. This is a deep shift from "I must do it" to "I can be here and trust." It involves stepping back so others can step forward.', practice: 'The Trust Exercise: in a relationship or at work, consciously step back and allow others to take the lead. Notice what happens. You may see capability in others you missed when you were in control.' },
      { stage: 5, title: 'Find Safety in Connection, Not Control', goal: 'Learn that safety comes from being held, not from holding everything.', description: 'The deepest shift is realizing that your safety is not in control. It is in your ability to be vulnerable, to trust, and to connect. This is terrifying. It requires letting go of the illusion that you are the one holding everything up. The ground will hold you. Others will hold you. You can rest.', practice: 'The Surrender Practice: once a day, consciously release control of something you are holding. It could be a plan, an expectation, a responsibility. Say to yourself: "I release this. It is not mine to hold."' },
    ],
    originalQuotes: [
      'Control is not safety—it is a cage you have painted to look like shelter.',
      'The person who must control everything is not strong. They are terrified.',
      'You cannot force the river to follow your map—you can only learn to navigate its currents.',
      'Your need to fix is not generosity—it is a refusal to tolerate the unknown.',
      'Others are not incompetent. They are being trained by your unwillingness to let go.',
      'If you always have to be in charge, you will always be the one who is exhausted.',
      'The opposite of control is not chaos—it is trust.',
      'You are not the one holding up the sky. You are the one who forgot how to rest under it.',
    ],
