    wisdomPerspectives: [
      { id: 'buddhism', label: 'Buddhism', question: 'What attachment is creating suffering here?', content: 'The Grief Bearer is attached to the past. They cling to the person they lost, refusing to let go because letting go feels like abandonment. But this attachment is not love—it is a form of suffering. Buddhism teaches that all things are impermanent. The person they lost is gone. Their grief is a form of clinging to something that no longer exists.', reflection: 'What would it mean to honor the person you lost without clinging to your grief? What would it feel like to release them—and still love them?' },
      { id: 'stoicism', label: 'Stoicism', question: 'What is within your control, and what is not?', content: 'The Grief Bearer has made themselves responsible for the impossible task of keeping the memory alive through suffering. But the death of a loved one is not within their control—and neither is the grief. The only thing they can control is their response to the grief. They can choose to let it transform them, not define them.', reflection: 'What would it mean to release your grip on the grief and let it soften? What would you be left with—and could that be enough?' },
      { id: 'somatic', label: 'Somatic Perspective', question: 'What sensations accompany the experience of carrying grief alone?', content: 'Some people notice chest tightness, shallow breathing, rounded shoulders, or a sense of heaviness when they think about their loss. These are common human experiences during grief and do not prove a specific cause or that emotion is stored in the body. Somatic reflection simply asks the person to notice what is present—without assigning one explanation or forcing the sensation to change.', reflection: 'If you could notice one sensation in your body right now without trying to fix it, what would it be? What would it feel like to let your breath be slightly fuller, even for one cycle?' },
      { id: 'chakra', label: 'Chakra and Yogic Tradition', question: 'How do chakra traditions symbolically describe grief?', content: 'Within chakra traditions, the heart center is symbolically associated with both grief and love. This perspective suggests that when loss is not given space to move, the heart may feel closed—not as a medical condition, but as a reflective image of protecting oneself from further pain. The throat center is symbolically tied to speaking what is true, and grief that remains unspoken may be held more tightly. As a reflective framework, these images can help someone ask where their grief is being held back, not as a diagnosis but as a question.', reflection: 'What would it feel like to open your heart to both grief and joy? What would it be like to let both exist at the same time?' },
      { id: 'symbolic', label: 'Symbolic Perspective', question: 'If this pattern were a story, what role would you be playing?', content: 'The Grief Bearer is the perpetual mourner—the one who never takes off the black veil. They are the guardian of the tomb, faithful to a memory, but forgetting that the departed would not want them to remain in mourning. The story is stuck in the funeral. The Grief Bearer has not heard the music that follows.', reflection: 'What would it mean to leave the funeral? What would happen if you allowed the ceremony to end and the life to resume?' },
      { id: 'mythology', label: 'Mythology', question: 'Which myth captures the inability to release grief?', content: 'The Grief Bearer is Persephone, trapped in the underworld for half the year, unable to fully return to the light. They are Demeter, who refused to let the seasons turn because she could not accept her daughter\'s loss. The myth asks: can we grieve and still let the world be reborn?', reflection: 'What would happen if you allowed the world to be reborn? What would it feel like to let spring come, even after a long winter?' },
    ],
    recoveryRoadmap: [
      { stage: 1, title: 'Acknowledge the Identification', goal: 'Recognize that you have fused your identity with grief.', description: 'The first stage is the hardest: seeing that you have become your grief. You are not just a person who is grieving—you are a person who has built an identity around the loss. This is a recognition, not a judgment. It is simply seeing the pattern.', practice: 'The Mirror Practice: ask yourself—who would I be without this grief? Write the first thing that comes to mind, even if it is painful.' },
      { stage: 2, title: 'Give Grief a Place', goal: 'Learn to contain grief so it doesn\'t contain you.', description: 'Grief needs a place. It cannot be everywhere, but it also cannot be nowhere. The Grief Bearer must create a container for the grief—a ritual, a time, a space—where they can go to honor it. Then they can leave it there and live the rest of their life.', practice: 'The Container Practice: each day, set aside a specific time to grieve—ten minutes, with a photo, a candle. Then close the container and move into the rest of your day.' },
      { stage: 3, title: 'Welcome Joy as a Companion', goal: 'Learn that joy can coexist with grief.', description: 'The Grief Bearer has believed that joy is a betrayal. The third stage is learning that joy and grief are not enemies—they are companions. You can feel the loss and still feel the sun on your face. You can honor the dead and still honor the living.', practice: 'The Joy Practice: each day, intentionally notice one small moment of joy. Give yourself permission to feel it without guilt.' },
      { stage: 4, title: 'Reconcile the Loss', goal: 'Learn to live with the loss, not against it.', description: 'The loss does not go away. The Grief Bearer learns to reconcile with it—to accept that the person is gone, that the grief will not disappear, and that they can still live fully. This is not letting go—it is accepting the shape of the loss.', practice: 'The Reconciliation Practice: write a letter to the person you lost. Tell them you are learning to live with the loss and that you will carry their memory with you—without being consumed by it.' },
      { stage: 5, title: 'Grieve and Move', goal: 'Move into life while still holding the grief.', description: 'The final stage is learning to move. You are not leaving the loss behind—you are integrating it. The grief is no longer the whole of you; it is part of you. You can carry it while also walking forward. You can mourn and live.', practice: 'The Moving Forward Practice: each day, take one action that moves your life forward—a connection, a project, a small step toward joy. Feel the grief and take the step anyway.' },
    ],
    originalQuotes: [
      'Grief is not a lifetime sentence. It is a depth that invites you to also know height.',
      'You are not betraying them by living. You are honoring them by carrying their memory in motion.',
      'Grief itself is not dangerous—but avoiding it can shape a life around the loss.',
      'They did not give you grief. They gave you love. The grief is the shape love takes when it cannot touch.',
      'You can hold the grief and still open the window.',
      'The dead do not ask you to stay in the grave with them.',
      'Grief is not the end of love—it is love transformed.',
      'You were not meant to carry this alone. And you were not meant to carry it forever.',
    ],
